course: dictionary
lesson: 10195
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning is meant. Timothy with a capital T is a name, like Tim or Timmy. Without a capital, timothy is a kind of grass.
explain: «In Dumbo, a whole row of elephant ladies panics at Timothy the mouse.» Here Timothy is the name of a little mouse. You can tell because a mouse is a character in the story, and Timothy is what the elephants call him.
explain: In the Bible, Timothy was a young friend and helper of Paul, and two letters in the New Testament were written to him. When you read «Timothy» in a church or Bible text, think of that person. When you read «timothy hay» in a pet book, think of grass.
fact: A name starts with a capital letter, so «Timothy» is a name for a boy or man.
fact: Short forms of Timothy are Tim and Timmy.
fact: In the Bible, Timothy was a young friend and helper of the apostle Paul.
fact: Timothy grass is grown for hay, which is fed to horses, rabbits and guinea pigs.
fact: Timothy hay is the main food for rabbits and guinea pigs.
exercise: «In Dumbo, a whole row of elephant ladies panics at Timothy the mouse.» Is Timothy a name here? | No, it is a kind of food | *Yes, it is the name of a mouse | No, it is a grass grown for hay | No, it is a type of horse
exercise: What is timothy hay the main food for? | Cats and dogs | Birds | *Rabbits and guinea pigs | Elephants
exercise: Who was Timothy in the Bible? | A king who built a great castle | A mouse in a cartoon | A ship's captain who sailed to Rome | *A young friend and helper of the apostle Paul
test: Jayden reads «Timothy» with a capital T in a story about a boy called Timothy. What does it mean? | A kind of grass | A food for horses | A church letter | *A boy's name, with short forms Tim and Timmy
test: «Rabbits need timothy hay every day.» What is timothy in this sentence? | A letter in the Bible | *A kind of grass grown for hay | A type of mouse | A name
exam: Why does context matter for the word timothy? | Because it never changes its meaning | Because the capital letter always means a name | Because it is a verb that means to hide | *Because the same word can be a name, a Bible helper or a grass, and the words around it show which one
